#### What the interviewer is really testing
- Using **CSS custom properties** as the theming foundation.
- Structuring **design tokens** (primitive → semantic → component).
- Handling **system preference, user override and persistence**.
- Avoiding **FOUC/flash of incorrect theme** with SSR.
- Accessibility (**contrast**) and scaling to **multiple brands**.

#### Key points
- **CSS variables** are inherited and can change at runtime without re-rendering React or regenerating CSS.
- **Token layers**: primitives (`--blue-600`) → semantic (`--color-bg`, `--color-text`, `--color-primary`) → optionally component tokens (`--button-bg`). Components use **semantic** tokens only.
- **System preference**: `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` and `color-scheme: light dark` so form controls and scrollbars match.
- **User choice**: three options (light, dark, system), stored in `localStorage` (or a cookie for SSR), applied as `data-theme` on `<html>`.
- **No flash**: set the theme attribute **before first paint** with a tiny inline script in `<head>` (or server-render it from a cookie).
- **`light-dark()`** CSS function simplifies paired values in modern browsers.
- Dark mode isn't just inverting colours: adjust **contrast, elevation (lighter surfaces), shadows, images and saturated colours**.
- **Multi-brand**: same semantic tokens, different values per brand (`[data-brand='x']`), possibly loaded as separate CSS files.

#### Model answer
"I'd build theming on CSS custom properties with semantic tokens, because switching a theme then means changing a few variables at the root, without touching component code or re-rendering.

**1. Tokens in layers:**
```css
:root {
  /* primitives: raw palette */
  --gray-50: #f9fafb; --gray-900: #111827;
  --blue-500: #3b82f6; --blue-400: #60a5fa;

  /* semantic tokens: what components use */
  --color-bg: var(--gray-50);
  --color-surface: #ffffff;
  --color-text: var(--gray-900);
  --color-text-muted: #4b5563;
  --color-primary: var(--blue-500);
  --color-border: #e5e7eb;
  --shadow-card: 0 1px 3px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.12);
  color-scheme: light;
}

[data-theme='dark'] {
  --color-bg: #0b0f17;
  --color-surface: #151b26;           /* elevated surfaces get lighter, not shadowier */
  --color-text: #e5e7eb;
  --color-text-muted: #9ca3af;
  --color-primary: var(--blue-400);   /* lighter blue keeps contrast on dark backgrounds */
  --color-border: #263042;
  --shadow-card: 0 0 0 1px var(--color-border);
  color-scheme: dark;                 /* native controls, scrollbars match */
}

.card { background: var(--color-surface); color: var(--color-text); box-shadow: var(--shadow-card); }
```
Components never reference raw colours, only semantic tokens, so adding a theme is just another block of values.

**2. System preference as the default:**
```css
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root:not([data-theme='light']) {
    /* same dark values as above (or share via a mixin/build step) */
  }
}
```
Modern alternative: `color: light-dark(#111827, #e5e7eb);` with `color-scheme: light dark`, which picks values automatically based on the active scheme.

**3. User toggle with three states (light, dark, system):**
```js
const KEY = 'theme';                                 // 'light' | 'dark' | 'system'
function applyTheme(choice) {
  const dark = choice === 'dark' ||
    (choice === 'system' && matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches);
  document.documentElement.dataset.theme = dark ? 'dark' : 'light';
}
function setTheme(choice) {
  try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, choice); } catch {}   // storage may be blocked
  applyTheme(choice);
}
matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').addEventListener('change', () => {
  if ((localStorage.getItem(KEY) ?? 'system') === 'system') applyTheme('system');
});
```
Offering 'system' matters: many users want the site to follow their OS automatically.

**4. Avoiding the flash of the wrong theme.** If the theme is applied after React loads, users see a white flash before switching to dark. Fix: a tiny blocking inline script in `<head>`, before CSS paints:
```html
<script>
  (function () {
    try {
      var c = localStorage.getItem('theme') || 'system';
      var dark = c === 'dark' || (c === 'system' && matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches);
      document.documentElement.dataset.theme = dark ? 'dark' : 'light';
    } catch (e) {}
  })();
</script>
```
With SSR, an alternative is storing the choice in a cookie so the server renders `<html data-theme="dark">` directly. Frameworks' theme libraries (e.g. next-themes) implement this pattern; in React, avoid reading localStorage during render to prevent hydration mismatches (the toggle's label can render after mount).

**5. Dark mode design details:**
- Don't pure-invert: use dark greys (not #000) to reduce eye strain and allow elevation.
- Re-check **contrast** for every token pair (WCAG AA: 4.5:1 for body text).
- Desaturate bright brand colours that vibrate on dark backgrounds.
- Images and illustrations: provide dark variants via `<picture>` with `media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)"`, or reduce brightness slightly.
- Shadows are less visible; use borders or lighter surfaces for elevation.

**6. Multi-brand theming:**
```css
[data-brand='acme']  { --color-primary: #e11d48; --font-display: 'Acme Sans'; --radius-md: 4px; }
[data-brand='globex'] { --color-primary: #059669; --font-display: 'Globex Serif'; --radius-md: 12px; }
```
Same components, different token values per brand, combinable with light/dark (`[data-brand='acme'][data-theme='dark']`). For large brands, generate token CSS from a single source (Style Dictionary or Tokens Studio exporting JSON) and load only the active brand's CSS file to avoid shipping every brand's values.

**Integration with tools:** Tailwind can map its colours to CSS variables (`bg-surface` → `var(--color-surface)`) so `dark:` variants aren't needed everywhere; CSS-in-JS themes can expose the same variables."

#### Another worked example: multi-brand e-commerce
"A retail group ran four brands on one React codebase. Initially brand styles were if-statements in components and every page shipped all four brands' styles. We moved to semantic tokens generated from a design-token JSON per brand, applied via `data-brand` and loaded as a separate CSS file per brand. Components stayed brand-agnostic, CSS per page dropped by ~70%, and launching a fifth brand became mostly a token file plus assets."

#### A third worked example: fixing the dark mode flash
"After launching dark mode, users reported a white flash on every navigation in our server-rendered app. The theme was applied in a `useEffect`. Moving the logic into an inline head script and persisting the choice in a cookie (so the server rendered the right `data-theme`) eliminated the flash and a hydration warning about the toggle icon."

#### Variations you might be asked
- "How do you theme third-party components?": override their CSS variables if exposed, or map their theme API to your tokens.
- "How do you test themes?": visual regression screenshots per theme, automated contrast checks on token pairs, and Storybook toolbars to switch themes.
- "Can CSS variables be animated?": registered properties via `@property` with a syntax can be transitioned; unregistered ones switch instantly.

#### Step-by-step framework
1. **CSS variables** as the foundation.
2. **Token layers** (primitive → semantic → component).
3. **System preference** and `color-scheme`.
4. **Three-state toggle** with persistence.
5. **No-flash initialisation** (inline script or cookie SSR).
6. **Design details, contrast and multi-brand scaling.**

#### Strong versus weak answer
| Weak answer | Strong answer |
|---|---|
| Toggle a dark class and override many rules | Semantic tokens swapped at the root |
| Only light/dark | Light, dark and system with persistence |
| Applies theme in useEffect | Inline head script / cookie SSR, no flash |
| Inverts colours | Elevation, contrast, desaturation, image variants |
| Hard-coded brand ifs | Token files per brand via data attributes |

#### Useful phrases
- "Components use semantic tokens, never raw colours."
- "The theme is set before first paint, so there's no flash."
- "I offer a 'system' option so the site follows the OS setting."
- "Brands share components and differ only in token values."

#### Common pitfalls
- **Flash of light theme.**
- **Hydration mismatches** from reading storage during render.
- **Poor contrast** in dark mode.
- **Hard-coded colours** bypassing tokens.

#### Follow-ups to expect (with sample answers)
- **"What does color-scheme do?"**
  "Tells the browser which schemes the page supports so built-in UI (form controls, scrollbars, default backgrounds) renders appropriately."
- **"Why not use a React context for theme colours?"**
  "Context changes re-render consumers; CSS variables switch instantly with no React work. Context can hold the user's choice, but values should be CSS."
- **"How do design tokens reach code?"**
  "Exported from design tools as JSON and transformed (Style Dictionary) into CSS variables, TS constants and platform formats."

#### What senior interviewers look for
- **Scalable token architecture.**
- **Polished UX** (no flash, system option).
- **Accessibility awareness.**

#### Practise it
1. Build a three-state theme toggle with no flash on reload.
2. Define primitive and semantic tokens for light and dark, and check contrast.
3. Explain multi-brand theming with tokens, out loud.

#### What the interviewer is really testing
- Knowledge of the **WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices** combobox pattern.
- Correct **roles, states and relationships** (`role="combobox"`, `aria-expanded`, `aria-controls`, `aria-activedescendant`, `role="listbox"`, `role="option"`, `aria-selected`).
- **Keyboard behaviour** (arrows, Enter, Escape, Home/End, Tab) and focus staying in the input.
- **Announcements** of result counts, plus async loading, empty states, and **testing** with real assistive tech.

#### Key points
- Prefer native first: `<select>` for simple fixed lists, `<input list>` with `<datalist>` for basic suggestions; build a custom combobox when you need rich options, async search, or styling native controls can't provide.
- **Structure**: an `<input role="combobox">` with `aria-expanded`, `aria-controls` (listbox ID), `aria-autocomplete="list"`, and `aria-activedescendant` pointing to the highlighted option; a `<ul role="listbox">` with `<li role="option" id aria-selected>`.
- **Focus stays in the input**; the "active" option is communicated via `aria-activedescendant`, so users can keep typing.
- **Keyboard**: Down/Up move the active option (opening the list), Enter selects, Escape closes (then clears), Home/End optional, Tab moves on (optionally selecting).
- **Labels**: visible `<label>` for the input; options have readable text.
- **Announcements**: a polite live region for "5 results available" and "No results"; selection confirmed.
- **Mouse/touch**: clicking options selects; prevent the input blur from closing the list before the click registers (use `onMouseDown` + `preventDefault`).
- Test with **keyboard, VoiceOver, NVDA, JAWS**, mobile screen readers; automated tests for roles and keyboard.
- Libraries (React Aria, Radix, Downshift, Headless UI) encode years of edge cases; use them unless you have a strong reason.

#### Model answer
"An autocomplete looks simple, but it's one of the hardest widgets to make accessible, so I'd follow the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices combobox pattern closely, and in production I'd strongly consider a tested library.

**First question: do we need a custom widget?** For a fixed list of 10 countries, a native `<select>` is fully accessible for free. A custom combobox is justified for searching thousands of items, async results, rich option content (avatars, descriptions) or multi-select chips.

**Structure and ARIA:**
```tsx
function Combobox({ label, options, onSelect }: { label: string; options: Item[]; onSelect: (i: Item) => void }) {
  const id = useId();
  const listId = `${id}-list`;
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  const [activeIndex, setActiveIndex] = useState(-1);

  const results = useMemo(
    () => options.filter((o) => o.label.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase())).slice(0, 50),
    [options, query],
  );
  const activeId = activeIndex >= 0 ? `${id}-opt-${results[activeIndex]?.id}` : undefined;

  function select(item: Item) {
    setQuery(item.label);
    setOpen(false);
    setActiveIndex(-1);
    onSelect(item);
  }

  function onKeyDown(e: React.KeyboardEvent<HTMLInputElement>) {
    switch (e.key) {
      case 'ArrowDown':
        e.preventDefault();
        setOpen(true);
        setActiveIndex((i) => Math.min(i + 1, results.length - 1));
        break;
      case 'ArrowUp':
        e.preventDefault();
        setActiveIndex((i) => Math.max(i - 1, 0));
        break;
      case 'Enter':
        if (open && activeIndex >= 0) { e.preventDefault(); select(results[activeIndex]); }
        break;
      case 'Escape':
        if (open) setOpen(false); else setQuery('');   // first Esc closes, second clears
        setActiveIndex(-1);
        break;
    }
  }

  return (
    <div className="combobox">
      <label htmlFor={`${id}-input`}>{label}</label>
      <input
        id={`${id}-input`}
        role="combobox"
        aria-expanded={open}
        aria-controls={listId}
        aria-autocomplete="list"
        aria-activedescendant={open ? activeId : undefined}
        value={query}
        onChange={(e) => { setQuery(e.target.value); setOpen(true); setActiveIndex(-1); }}
        onKeyDown={onKeyDown}
        onBlur={() => setOpen(false)}
        autoComplete="off"
      />
      {open && (
        <ul id={listId} role="listbox" aria-label={label}>
          {results.map((item, i) => (
            <li
              key={item.id}
              id={`${id}-opt-${item.id}`}
              role="option"
              aria-selected={i === activeIndex}
              className={i === activeIndex ? 'active' : undefined}
              onMouseDown={(e) => e.preventDefault()}      // keep focus in the input so blur doesn't close first
              onClick={() => select(item)}
              onMouseMove={() => setActiveIndex(i)}
            >
              {item.label}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
      <div role="status" aria-live="polite" className="visually-hidden">
        {open ? (results.length ? `${results.length} results available. Use up and down arrows to choose.` : 'No results') : ''}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Why these pieces matter:**
- **`role="combobox"` on the input** tells assistive tech this text field controls a popup list.
- **`aria-expanded`** announces whether suggestions are showing.
- **`aria-controls`** links the input to the listbox.
- **`aria-activedescendant`**: focus stays in the input (so typing continues to work), while the screen reader announces the highlighted option as if focused. The referenced option must have a stable `id` and exist in the DOM.
- **`aria-selected`** on the active option; visual highlight must match.
- **Live region** announces result counts after typing (debounced for async search), since users otherwise don't know suggestions appeared.
- **`onMouseDown` preventDefault** stops the input losing focus before the click handler runs, a classic bug where clicking an option just closes the list.
- **Visible label** via `<label htmlFor>`.

**Keyboard behaviour (APG):**
| Key | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| Typing | Filters and opens the list |
| Down / Up | Moves the active option (Down opens if closed) |
| Enter | Selects the active option, closes the list |
| Escape | Closes the list; if already closed, clears input |
| Tab | Moves focus onward (optionally accepting the active option) |
| Home / End | Move caret in input (or first/last option, per design) |

**Async search additions:**
- Debounce requests, cancel stale ones (AbortController) so older responses don't replace newer results.
- Announce 'Loading…' politely, then results; show a visible loading indicator.
- Handle errors with a message inside the popup and in the live region.

**Visual and pointer details:**
- Highlight the active option with more than colour (background plus outline), 3:1 contrast.
- Scroll the active option into view (`scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest' })`) when navigating long lists.
- Large enough touch targets on mobile; ensure the popup isn't obscured by the on-screen keyboard.
- Render the popup in a portal if it may be clipped by overflow, keeping the ARIA relationships intact.

**Testing:**
- **Automated**: Testing Library queries by role (`getByRole('combobox')`, `getByRole('option', { name: 'France' })`), keyboard interactions with `userEvent`, axe checks.
- **Manual**: keyboard-only; VoiceOver (Safari, iOS) and NVDA/JAWS (Chrome/Firefox on Windows); behaviour differs between screen readers, which is why APG patterns and libraries matter.

**When to use a library:** almost always in production. React Aria's `useComboBox`, Downshift, Radix or Headless UI handle screen reader quirks, virtualised lists, IME composition (typing Chinese/Japanese), mobile behaviour, RTL and internationalisation. I'd hand-build only for learning, or when a library can't meet a specific requirement, and then test across screen readers."

#### Another worked example: replacing a broken autocomplete
"An address lookup used a custom dropdown of divs: arrow keys didn't work, screen readers announced nothing as results appeared, and clicking an option sometimes closed the list without selecting. We replaced it with React Aria's combobox hooks styled with our design system, added a polite results count, and AbortController for request races. Accessibility audit issues for the checkout dropped from 11 to 0 for that step, and keyboard users could finally complete addresses."

#### A third worked example: tabs, the simpler pattern
"For an accessible tabs component, the APG pattern is: `role="tablist"` containing `role="tab"` buttons with `aria-selected` and `aria-controls`, panels with `role="tabpanel"` and `aria-labelledby`, roving tabindex so only the active tab is in the Tab order, arrow keys to move between tabs, and Tab to move into the panel. Deciding between automatic activation (arrow keys switch panels) and manual (Enter/Space) depends on whether panels load quickly."

#### Variations you might be asked
- "What's the difference between a combobox and a menu?": a combobox is an input with a popup of values to choose; a menu offers actions (like an app menu), with different roles and keyboard rules.
- "How do you handle multi-select?": `aria-multiselectable="true"` on the listbox, selected chips with remove buttons that have accessible names, and announcements on add/remove.
- "What about virtualised option lists?": `aria-setsize` and `aria-posinset` on options so screen readers know the total, while only rendering visible items.

#### Step-by-step framework
1. **Question the need** for a custom widget.
2. **Structure**: label, input with combobox role, listbox, options.
3. **States and relationships**: expanded, controls, activedescendant, selected.
4. **Keyboard interactions** per APG.
5. **Announcements, async, pointer details.**
6. **Testing** and library choice.

#### Strong versus weak answer
| Weak answer | Strong answer |
|---|---|
| Div dropdown with click handlers | APG combobox pattern with correct roles |
| Moves DOM focus into options | aria-activedescendant keeps focus in input |
| No announcements | Polite live region for counts and loading |
| List closes before click registers | mousedown preventDefault |
| Hand-built for production without testing | Library or cross-screen-reader testing |

#### Useful phrases
- "If a native select works, I don't build a combobox."
- "Focus stays in the input; aria-activedescendant tells the screen reader which option is active."
- "I announce the number of results so users know suggestions appeared."
- "In production I'd use React Aria or Downshift, because they handle the screen reader quirks."

#### Common pitfalls
- **Mismatched visual and ARIA active states.**
- **Missing ids** for activedescendant targets.
- **Race conditions** in async results.
- **Ignoring IME composition** for non-Latin input.

#### Follow-ups to expect (with sample answers)
- **"Why not move real focus to options?"**
  "Users need to keep typing; moving focus would break text entry and complicate returning to the input."
- **"How do you handle IME input?"**
  "Ignore Enter/arrow handling during composition (check `event.nativeEvent.isComposing`) so users can confirm characters."
- **"How do you test with screen readers efficiently?"**
  "A short script of tasks per widget, run with VoiceOver and NVDA on each release of the design system component."

#### What senior interviewers look for
- **Pattern-level knowledge.**
- **Attention to real edge cases.**
- **Pragmatic library judgement.**

#### Practise it
1. Build the combobox above and test it with VoiceOver or NVDA.
2. Add async search with debounce, abort and loading announcements.
3. Explain aria-activedescendant, out loud.

#### What the interviewer is really testing
- Understanding **why responses can arrive out of order** and overwrite newer data.
- Using **effect cleanup** correctly to ignore or cancel stale requests.
- **AbortController** usage, including handling `AbortError`.
- Knowing that **data-fetching libraries** (TanStack Query, SWR, framework loaders, RSC) solve this and more.
- Awareness of **StrictMode** double-invocation in development.

#### Key points
- If `userId` changes from 1 to 2 quickly, request 1 may resolve **after** request 2; without protection, the UI shows user 1's data for user 2: a **race condition**.
- The effect **cleanup** runs before the next effect (and on unmount): use it to mark the previous request as stale or abort it.
- **Ignore flag**: `let ignore = false; ... if (!ignore) setData(...); return () => { ignore = true; }`.
- **AbortController**: cancel the network request in cleanup; catch and ignore `AbortError`. Saves bandwidth and server work.
- Also handle **loading and error states**, and reset data appropriately when the key changes.
- **StrictMode** (development) mounts, unmounts and remounts effects, so you'll see a request start and abort; correct cleanup makes this harmless.
- "Can't set state on unmounted component" warnings are gone in React 18, but stale updates are still bugs.
- Prefer **TanStack Query/SWR** or framework data loading (Server Components, route loaders) for caching, dedupe, retries and race handling.

#### Model answer
"The race happens because network responses don't come back in the order requests were sent.

**The buggy version:**
```tsx
function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState<User | null>(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)
      .then((r) => r.json())
      .then(setUser);              // sets whatever arrives, even if it's outdated
  }, [userId]);
  return user ? <Profile user={user} /> : <Spinner />;
}
```
Timeline when switching quickly from user 1 to user 2:
```
t=0    request A: /users/1 (slow: 800 ms)
t=100  userId → 2, request B: /users/2 (fast: 200 ms)
t=300  B resolves → setUser(user 2)   ✓
t=800  A resolves → setUser(user 1)   ✗ the page now shows user 1 while the URL says user 2
```

**Fix 1: ignore stale responses with cleanup:**
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  let ignore = false;
  setUser(null);                                   // or keep previous data with a stale indicator
  fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)
    .then((r) => r.json())
    .then((data) => { if (!ignore) setUser(data); });
  return () => { ignore = true; };                 // runs before the next effect and on unmount
}, [userId]);
```
When `userId` changes, React runs the previous effect's cleanup, marking that request's result as stale; only the latest effect's result is applied.

**Fix 2: abort the request with AbortController:**
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  setStatus('loading');
  fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`, { signal: controller.signal })
    .then((r) => {
      if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${r.status}`);
      return r.json();
    })
    .then((data) => { setUser(data); setStatus('success'); })
    .catch((err) => {
      if (err.name === 'AbortError') return;      // expected when superseded or unmounted
      setError(err);
      setStatus('error');
    });
  return () => controller.abort();
}, [userId]);
```
Benefits over the flag: the browser actually cancels the request (saving bandwidth and possibly server work), and it's the same mechanism used for timeouts. Remember: `fetch` rejects with `AbortError` on abort, which must not be shown as an error.

With async/await inside an effect:
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  (async () => {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
      const data = await res.json();
      setData(data);
    } catch (e) {
      if ((e as Error).name !== 'AbortError') setError(e as Error);
    }
  })();
  return () => controller.abort();
}, [url]);
```
(The effect callback itself can't be async because it must return the cleanup function, not a promise.)

**StrictMode in development:** React mounts the component, runs effects, simulates unmount (runs cleanups), then runs effects again. You'll see the first request aborted and a second one made. That's intentional: it proves cleanup works. Don't 'fix' it by removing StrictMode or adding refs to skip the second run.

**Setting state after unmount:** React 18 removed the warning, but the underlying issue (doing work for a component that no longer exists) still matters; aborting in cleanup handles it.

**Better: use a data-fetching library or the framework:**
```tsx
const { data: user, isPending, error } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['user', userId],
  queryFn: ({ signal }) => fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`, { signal }).then((r) => r.json()),
});
```
TanStack Query (or SWR) handles races (results are keyed by `userId`, so a stale response can't overwrite the current key's data), caching, deduplication of identical requests, retries, background refetching, and passes an AbortSignal. In Next.js App Router, fetching in Server Components or route loaders avoids client-side effect fetching for initial data entirely. The React docs themselves recommend not writing fetch-in-effect by hand for most apps.

**Other details worth mentioning:**
- Decide UX on key change: clear old data (show spinner) or keep it with a 'stale' indicator (`placeholderData: keepPreviousData` in TanStack Query).
- Debounce for search inputs to reduce requests; abort handles ordering.
- Server-side: aborting the client request may not stop server work unless the server listens for disconnects."

#### Another worked example: a chat conversation switcher
"Support agents switching quickly between conversations sometimes saw messages from the previous customer in the new conversation, a privacy issue. The messages effect had no cleanup. Adding AbortController-based cleanup fixed the race; we then moved to TanStack Query keyed by conversation ID, which also cached recently viewed conversations so switching back was instant."

#### A third worked example: StrictMode confusion
"A developer noticed two requests per page load in development and added a `useRef` flag to skip the second effect run. In production (no double invocation) it worked, but it broke when the component remounted legitimately (switching tabs), skipping the fetch. Removing the hack and adding proper abort cleanup made both development and production behave correctly."

#### Variations you might be asked
- "How do you handle races without AbortController (e.g. non-fetch promises)?": the ignore flag, or a request counter/ID comparing against the latest.
- "What about race conditions with user actions (double submit)?": disable buttons while pending, idempotency keys on the server, and ignore duplicate submissions.
- "Can useEffect be async?": no; use an inner async function and return a cleanup.

#### Step-by-step framework
1. **Explain the out-of-order response race** with a timeline.
2. **Fix with ignore flag** in cleanup.
3. **Fix with AbortController**, handling AbortError.
4. **Loading/error/UX on key change.**
5. **StrictMode explanation.**
6. **Recommend libraries/framework data loading.**

#### Strong versus weak answer
| Weak answer | Strong answer |
|---|---|
| "Use async/await" | Explains out-of-order responses |
| No cleanup | Ignore flag or abort in cleanup |
| Shows AbortError as failure | Filters AbortError |
| Disables StrictMode | Understands double effects as a cleanup check |
| Hand-rolls everything | Recommends TanStack Query/SWR/framework loaders |

#### Useful phrases
- "Responses can arrive in a different order from requests."
- "The effect cleanup marks the previous request as stale or aborts it."
- "An aborted fetch rejects with AbortError, which isn't a real error."
- "In production code I'd use TanStack Query, which keys results by the request."

#### Common pitfalls
- **No cleanup in fetching effects.**
- **Treating AbortError as an error.**
- **Async effect callbacks.**
- **Hacks to skip StrictMode double effects.**

#### Follow-ups to expect (with sample answers)
- **"Why does the cleanup run before the next effect?"**
  "React guarantees each effect's cleanup runs before re-running the effect with new dependencies, so resources from the previous run can be released."
- **"Does aborting stop the server work?"**
  "Not necessarily; the server must observe the closed connection and cancel work explicitly."
- **"What's keepPreviousData?"**
  "A TanStack Query option to keep showing the last successful data while fetching a new key, avoiding spinners on pagination."

#### What senior interviewers look for
- **Clear understanding of async timing.**
- **Correct cleanup patterns.**
- **Pragmatic library choices.**

#### Practise it
1. Reproduce the race with artificial delays, then fix with both techniques.
2. Rewrite the component with TanStack Query and compare.
3. Explain why StrictMode runs effects twice, out loud.

body:
Review this component. List everything that's wrong or missing, explain the impact of each issue, and write an improved version.

```jsx
function Search() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch(`/api?q=${query}`).then(r => r.json()).then(setResults);
  }, [query]);
  return <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />;
}
```

answer:
#### What the interviewer is really testing
- A **systematic code review**: correctness, performance, UX, accessibility, security.
- Spotting **race conditions** and missing **cleanup**.
- Recognising missing **debounce, empty-query handling, encoding, error and loading states**.
- Writing a **clean improved version** and knowing when to use a library.

#### Key points
- **Results are never rendered** (bug or unfinished).
- **Race condition**: out-of-order responses can show results for an older query; no **abort/cleanup**.
- **No debounce**: a request per keystroke, hammering the API.
- **Empty query** still fetches (`/api?q=`), and very short queries may be pointless.
- **No URL encoding**: `&`, `#`, `?`, spaces break the URL or inject params; use `URLSearchParams`/`encodeURIComponent`.
- **No HTTP error handling**: fetch doesn't reject on 4xx/5xx; `r.json()` may throw on HTML error pages.
- **No loading, empty or error states.**
- **Accessibility**: input has no label; results changes aren't announced; consider combobox semantics if it's an autocomplete.
- **Unhandled promise rejections**; setting state for stale/unmounted requests.
- Other: no caching (repeated queries refetch), state for derived data, StrictMode double requests (fine with cleanup), possibly sync query to URL.
- Better: debounce + AbortController, or TanStack Query with `keepPreviousData`.

#### Model answer
"I'd go through it as I would a real code review, starting with correctness, then performance, UX, accessibility and security.

**1. Results are never rendered.** `results` is stored but the component only returns the input. Either it's unfinished or a bug; I'd ask, and render a results list.

**2. Race condition (correctness).** Typing 'react' fires requests for 'r', 're', 'rea', 'reac', 'react'. Responses can arrive in any order; if 'rea' resolves last, the UI shows results for 'rea' while the input says 'react'. The effect has no cleanup to ignore or abort stale requests.

**3. No debouncing (performance/cost).** One request per keystroke. A 10-character query sends 10 requests, wasting server capacity and bandwidth, and possibly triggering rate limits. Debounce by ~250–300 ms.

**4. Empty and short queries.** On mount (query `''`) it fetches `/api?q=` immediately; clearing the input fetches again. Skip the request (and clear results) for empty queries, maybe require 2+ characters.

**5. No URL encoding (correctness/security).** A query like `shoes & socks` or `50%` or `a#b` produces a malformed URL or injects extra parameters (`q=a&admin=true`). Use `URLSearchParams` or `encodeURIComponent`.

**6. No error handling.** `fetch` only rejects on network failure; a 500 response still resolves, and `r.json()` may throw if the server returns HTML. There's no `.catch`, so failures become unhandled promise rejections and the user sees nothing.

**7. No loading or empty states (UX).** Users can't tell whether a search is running, failed, or returned nothing.

**8. Accessibility.** The input has no label (screen readers announce 'edit text' with no context); result updates aren't announced. If it's an autocomplete with a dropdown, it needs the combobox pattern; for a results list, a polite live region with the result count.

**9. Other improvements:** cache repeated queries (TanStack Query), keep previous results visible while loading the next query to avoid flicker, consider syncing the query to the URL (shareable searches, back button), and in StrictMode the effect runs twice in development, which is fine once cleanup exists.

**Improved version (hand-written):**
```tsx
type Result = { id: string; title: string };
type Status = 'idle' | 'loading' | 'success' | 'error';

function useDebouncedValue<T>(value: T, delay: number) {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delay);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [value, delay]);
  return debounced;
}

export function Search() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const debouncedQuery = useDebouncedValue(query.trim(), 300);
  const [results, setResults] = useState<Result[]>([]);
  const [status, setStatus] = useState<Status>('idle');

  useEffect(() => {
    if (debouncedQuery.length < 2) {
      setResults([]);
      setStatus('idle');
      return;
    }
    const controller = new AbortController();
    setStatus('loading');
    const params = new URLSearchParams({ q: debouncedQuery });

    fetch(`/api/search?${params}`, { signal: controller.signal })
      .then((res) => {
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Search failed (${res.status})`);
        return res.json() as Promise<{ results: Result[] }>;
      })
      .then((data) => { setResults(data.results); setStatus('success'); })
      .catch((err) => {
        if (err.name === 'AbortError') return;      // superseded by a newer query or unmounted
        setStatus('error');
      });

    return () => controller.abort();               // cancel stale requests: fixes the race
  }, [debouncedQuery]);

  return (
    <div role="search">
      <label htmlFor="site-search">Search products</label>
      <input
        id="site-search"
        type="search"
        value={query}
        onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)}
        autoComplete="off"
      />
      <p role="status" aria-live="polite" className="visually-hidden">
        {status === 'loading' ? 'Searching…'
          : status === 'success' ? `${results.length} results`
          : status === 'error' ? 'Search failed. Please try again.' : ''}
      </p>
      {status === 'error' && <p className="error">Something went wrong. Try again.</p>}
      {status === 'success' && results.length === 0 && <p>No results for “{debouncedQuery}”.</p>}
      <ul aria-busy={status === 'loading'}>
        {results.map((r) => <li key={r.id}>{r.title}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}
```

